        "text": "While in this work we are interested in modeling sequences of vectors, let us first review how state space models define a sequence-to-sequence map for 1-D sequences.\nState Spaces A discretized state space, parameterized by a state matrix A \u2208 RN\u00d7N , vectors B \u2208 RN\u00d71, C \u2208 R1\u00d7N and a sample time \u2206 \u2208 R>0 defines a sequence-to-sequence map from input\n(u0, . . . , uL\u22121) = u \u2208 RL to output (y0, . . . , yL\u22121) = y \u2208 RL via the recurrence1, xk = Axk\u22121 +Buk , yk = Cxk\nA = eA\u2206 , B = (A \u2212 I)A\u22121B , C = C . (1)\nAssuming x\u22121 = 0 for simplicity, the above recurrence can be explicitly unrolled as\nyk = k\n\u2211 j=0 CA j B \u22c5 uk\u2212j . (2)\nFor convenience, the scalars CA k B are gathered to define the SSM kernel K \u2208 RL as\nK = (CB,CAB, . . . , CAL\u22121B) = ( CeA\u22c5k\u2206(eA\u2206 \u2212 I)A\u22121B )0\u2264k<L, (3)\nwhere the last equality follows by substituting the values of A, B, C from Equation 1. Hence,\nyk = k\n\u2211 j=0 Kj \u22c5 uk\u2212j . (4)\nwhereKj denotes the value of the kernel at position j. Given an input sequence u \u2208 R L, it is possible to compute the output y \u2208 RL sequentially via the recurrence in Equation 1. While this property is highly desirable for autoregressive decoding, a sequential computation is prohibitively slow to train with long inputs and, instead, Equation 4 can be used to compute all elements of y in parallel, provided we have already computed K.\nComputing y from u and K is easy. Given an input sequence u \u2208 RL and the SSM kernel K \u2208 RL, naively using Equation 4 for computing y would requireO(L2) multiplications. Fortunately, this can be done much more efficiently by observing that for the univariate polynomials\nK(z) = L\u22121\n\u2211 i=0 Kiz i and u(z) =\nL\u22121 \u2211 i=0 uiz i ,\nyk is the coefficient of z k in the polynomialK(z) \u22c5u(z), i.e. all yk\u2019s can be computed simultaneously by multiplying two degree L\u2212 1 polynomials. It is well-known that this can be done in O(L log(L)) time via Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) (Cormen et al., 2009). We denote this fast computation of Equation 4 via the discrete convolution as\ny = K \u2217c u . (5)\nDiagonal State Spaces The challenging part is computing K itself as it involves computing L distinct matrix powers (Equation 3). Gupta et al. (2022) observed that the state matrix A can be assumed to be diagonal without loss in performance, thereby allowing a straighforward computation of K. Their DSSEXP model (DSS from hereon) assumes A to be a diagonal matrix diag(\u03bb1, . . . , \u03bbN) and assumes B = (1)1\u2264i\u2264N . DSS has parameters \u039bre,\u039bim \u2208 RN , C \u2208 CN and \u2206log \u2208 R. The diagonal of A (i.e. (\u03bb1, . . . , \u03bbN)) is computed as \u2212elementwise-exp(\u039bre)+ i \u22c5\u039bim where i = \u221a \u22121 and \u2206 is computed as exp(\u2206log) \u2208 R>0. For this parameterization, the kernel (Equation 3) can be computed as a matrix-vector product\nK = (C \u2217 ((e\u03bbi\u2206 \u2212 1)/\u03bbi)1\u2264i\u2264N)1\u00d7N \u22c5 elementwise-exp(PN\u00d7L) (6)\nwhere Pi,k = \u03bbik\u2206 and * denotes elementwise multiplication. This formally defines a linear sequence-to-sequence map for 1-D sequences. In the case of sequences of H-dimensional vectors, state space models are applied individually on the H features as follows.\n1Discretization used in Equation 1 relies on zero order hold (ZOH) which assumes a constant value between sampled times. We also experimented with other discretization methods such as bilinear transform but did not see much difference in performance.\nEach DSS layer receives a length-L sequence u of H-dimensional vectors as input, i.e., u \u2208 RH\u00d7L, and produces an output y \u2208 RH\u00d7L. The parameters of the layer are \u039bre,\u039bim \u2208 R N , \u2206log \u2208 R H and C \u2208 CH\u00d7N . For each coordinate h = 1, . . . ,H , a